this used to be a road until it  disappeared underwater mid last year now  the only way to get around in this part  of south sudan is by boats and canoes  it's the worst flood this region has  seen in 60 years in this area every home  is abandoned  families had no choice but to leave  people like noel mccoy who arrived here  in july her children and extended family  now sleep in this makeshift house not  sure if and when they will be able to go  back home  my house is still under water there are  a lot of snakes and reptiles the place  is still a river it's no longer home so  how can i go back  the landscape has changed there is now  more water and less dry land the united  nations says close to 800 000 people  have been affected  many of them can only be reached by boat  they urgently need shelter clean water  and have to rely on food rations from  humanitarian agencies even before  the effects of this climate change  crisis even before that because of the  ongoing conflict in south sudan people  were already food insecure  before climate change there was about 50  to 60 percent of the population in need  of food assistance now with this extreme  weather patterns  there's about 70 to 75 so we're seeing a  decline in people's ability to put food  on the table and to feed their children  and it is unfortunately a ticking bomb  flood waters have destroyed crops  blocked access to fields and reduced  grazing land last year this area was dry  there were a few homes and animals here  then it started to rain and it began to  flood people built this dike to try and  keep the water back and some of them had  to move over this side to start over  this country is no stranger to floods  but they are now becoming more frequent  when you get displaced people like now  we have in mangala  they are also having a lot of issues  with  the  host communities because those people  are coming with their livestock and  these people are agriculture agriculture  so there's a lot of conflict so this  impact of climate change is now going to  make us have internally displaced people  even refugees  not from  armed conflict but from the impacts of  climate it's the third straight year of  severe flooding of the white nile in  south sudan after unusually high  rainfall in upstream countries health  officials fear an increase in waterborne  diseases including malaria  people out here are saying they need  help and they need it fast  hari mutasa al jazeera jungle a state  south sudan
 

